The '''Urban Redevelopment Project''' ({{lang-bl|Projecto de Reurbanitzación Urbana}}), popularly known as the '''Casajuan Plan''' ({{lang-bl|Plano Casajuan}}) or '''Casajuan Initiative''' is a set of policy initiatives by the [[Confederal Congress]] with the overarching aim of encouraging [[W:Adaptive reuse|adaptive reuse]] and reversing {{W|urban decay}} and {{W|suburbanization}} as {{W|remote work}} and {{W|e-commerce}} reduce the need for commuting and office and retail space. An impact assessed plan was developed between 2015 and 2019, in order to determine how to achieve redevelopment of abandoned or vacant office and retail space in {{W|urban centers}} into {{W|mixed-use development}}s in order to alleviate the [[Baliscan housing shortage]]. Its implementation has been tied into the updated [[Low Impact Initiative]] and [[Green Protocol]].

The plan calls for the identification of urban property left vacant for more than two years, which is to be transferred to the control local planning and development boards, which determines the best course of action by assessing community needs and sentiment. As per confederal guidance, all residential conversions must be at least 85% {{W|affordable housing|reserved as affordable}} (meaning [[W:Public ownership|public]] or [[W:Social ownership|social ownership]]) under [[Communes of Balisca|communal]] and [[Cantons of Balisca|cantonal]] [[Housing authority (Balisca)|housing authorities]]. The project is then voted on by the community boards, before making its way up to the commune council and ultimately the cantonal council.

== Background ==
The origins of the Casajuan Plan are rooted in the significant social and environmental challenges of the mid-20th century. From the 1950s onward, [[Castejón]] and other cities saw a period of intense {{W|verticalization}}, with the capital's 50-meter height restriction imposed after the [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake|1924 earthquake]] being relaxed. This encouraged a boom in the construction of increasingly taller high-rise buildings, culminating in the controversial 2018 [[Ampurias Tower]], a mixed-use [[W:Supertall skyscraper|supertall skyscraper]], which came to symbolize a form of development that was often aesthetically jarring and environmentally insensitive. This era of unchecked vertical growth caused increased public concern over its effects on urban ecology, such as changes to air circulation (dubbed as 'wall effect') and light pollution. The controversy surrounding the approval of the Ampurias Tower propelled the movement against continued skyscraper development into the national spotlight, and the seven years since it was completed 50,479 of Balisca's 57,681 [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] have either introduced restrictions on supertalls or banned them entirely. 

The rapid expansion of the internet as a new medium of communication, and the revival of the [[W:Library economy|library economy]] concept from the late 1990s onward presented the confederal government with both an opportunity and a challenge. In line with the [[Low Impact Initiative]] and [[Green Protocol]], the government adopted several initiatives aimed at shifting society towards {{W|telecommuting}} — not merely as a convenience, but as a deliberate strategy to confront and reverse continued office space construction and urban sprawl. The [[W:COVID-19 pandemic|COVID-19 pandemic]] and subsequent {{W|COVID-19 lockdowns|lockdowns}} dramatically accelerated this transition, producing near-total economic interruption followed by unprecedented drops in office occupancy, while advances in {{W|Industrial automation|automation}} and {{W|Remote work|remote work}} rendered vast quantities of commercial space permanently surplus. 

== Provisions and principles ==
Its primary provisions and principles include:

* '''Adaptive reuse and urban conversion:''' Vacant or underutilized commercial and office space is converted into {{W|mixed-use development}} or fully residential development as the primary strategy for urban growth. This is a conscious effort to increase the density and livability of existing urban areas while simultaneously protecting surrounding agricultural land and natural ecosystems from {{W|urban sprawl}} — and, critically, to ensure that the existing built fabric is repurposed rather than abandoned.

* '''Sustainable construction''': The law promotes and incentivizes the use of sustainable building materials, such as [[W:Mass timber|mass timber]], which is central to the recent push for "[[W:Plyscraper|plyscrapers]]" and low-carbon construction. This aligns with Balisca's [[W:Eco-socialism|eco-socialist]] and regenerative economic principles.

* '''Integrated green infrastructure and space''': Requires redeveloped properties to integrate [[W:Green infrastructure|green infrastructure]] and achieve net ecological gain. Measures such as {{W|permeable pavement}}, {{W|rooftop garden|rooftop gardens}}, living walls, and {{W|biophilic design}} are mandated to combat urban pollution, manage {{W|stormwater}}, and improve air circulation (addressing issues like the "wall effect"). All redevelopment projects must achieve a net gain in ecological function over the pre-conversion state, replacing {{W|impervious surface|impervious surfaces}} and biologically inert facades with integrated living systems.

* '''Housing and public space''': It guides the development of [[W:Public housing|social housing]] and [[W:Housing cooperative|housing cooperatives]], ensuring that new construction is not driven by private profit but serves the "[[Basic Law: Housing and Shelter|universal right to housing]]" and contributes to the creation of vibrant, communal public spaces.

